window.docContents[170].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-vector-spaces/#Chapter2___Definition2___11___1":{"contents":"Definition 2.11.1. The tensor product V \\otimes W of vector spaces V and W over a field k is the quotient of the space V * W whose basis is given by formal symbols v \\otimes w, v \\in V, w \\in W, by the subspace spanned by the elements\n\n(v_1 + v_2) \\otimes w - v_1 \\otimes w - v_2 \\otimes w,\nv \\otimes (w_1 + w_2) - v \\otimes w_1 - v \\otimes w_2,\nav \\otimes w - a(v \\otimes w),\nv \\otimes aw - a(v \\otimes w),\n\nwhere v \\in V, w \\in W, a \\in k.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.11. Tensor products\tTensor product of vector spaces","header":"Tensor product of vector spaces","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-vector-spaces/#Chapter2___Definition2___11___1"},"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page/#Frontmatter___BlankPage2":{"contents":"[This page is intentionally left blank.]\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\tFrontmatter\tBlank Page 2 — Blank page","header":"Blank Page 2 — Blank page","id":"/Frontmatter/Blank-Page-2-___-Blank-page/#Frontmatter___BlankPage2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Chapter7___Example7___9___5":{"contents":"Example 7.9.5. The category of representations of a finite group G over a field of characteristic not dividing |G| (or 0) is semisimple.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.9. Exact functors\tRepresentations of finite groups as semisimple category","header":"Representations of finite groups as semisimple category","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Chapter7___Example7___9___5"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Section-7___9___-Exact-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.9. Exact functors\tSection 7.9: Exact functors","header":"Section 7.9: Exact functors","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Section-7___9___-Exact-functors/#Chapter7___Introduction_7___9"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Chapter9___Problem9___5___3":{"contents":"Problem 9.5.3. (i) Show that there is a natural bijection between blocks of \\mathcal{C} and indecomposable central idempotents e_k of A (i.e., ones that cannot be nontrivially split in a sum of two central idempotents), such that \\mathcal{C}_k is the category of finite dimensional e_k A-modules.\n\n(ii) Show that any indecomposable object of \\mathcal{C} lies in some \\mathcal{C}_k and that \\operatorname{Hom}(M, N) = 0 if M \\in \\mathcal{C}_k, N \\in \\mathcal{C}_l, k \\neq l. Thus, \\mathcal{C} = \\bigoplus_{k \\in B} \\mathcal{C}_k.\n\n(iii) Determine the blocks in the category of left A-modules for A = k[S_3], where k is of characteristic 2.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t9. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\t9.5. Blocks\tBlocks and indecomposable central idempotents","header":"Blocks and indecomposable central idempotents","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___5___-Blocks/Blocks-and-indecomposable-central-idempotents/#Chapter9___Problem9___5___3"}});
